WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.600
In this video, you create a virtual machine with two scheduling rules: a node rule limits its hosts, and an anti-affinity rule keeps it apart from another virtual machine.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:18.000
The virtual machine demo-sched-1 runs on ser5-green. The second virtual machine must not share its host.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.430
Select Create.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.720
Choose the demo namespace,

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.590
and name it demo-sched-2.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.990
Give it one CPU

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.590
and two gigabytes of memory.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.970
On the Volumes tab,

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:38.610
select the openSUSE Leap image for the boot disk.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:45.880
Open the Node Scheduling tab. By default, the virtual machine can run on any available node.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.730
Select Run virtual machine on nodes matching scheduling rules.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:56.930
Add a node selector. Its priority stays Required, so the rule must be met.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.210
Add a rule.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:04.140
For the key, use the node label kubernetes.io/hostname.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:13.320
Keep the operator in list, and enter ser5-green and ser5-purple. The virtual machine can run only on these two hosts.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.990
Next, open the Virtual Machine Scheduling tab.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.800
Add a workload selector.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.150
Set the type to Anti-Affinity, and keep the priority Required.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:29.110
Add a rule that selects the other virtual machine.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:35.110
Every virtual machine carries the label harvesterhci.io/vmName.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.030
Set its value to demo-sched-1.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:45.210
With the topology key kubernetes.io/hostname, the two virtual machines never share a host.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.230
Select Create.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:02:01.510
demo-sched-2 runs on ser5-purple. The node rule allowed ser5-green and ser5-purple, and anti-affinity ruled out ser5-green.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:10.182
Both rules are part of the virtual machine and also apply when it restarts or migrates. Next, you put a host into maintenance mode.
